  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 115 AM EDT SUN SEP 6 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Monday evening with the latest model \nsuite analysis showing lingering troughing over the northeastern \nCONUS. Meanwhile, upper-level ridging is shifting east across the \nMississippi River ahead of a trough transiting from the Pacific \nNorthwest over the northern Rockies. Translating down to the \nsurface, high pressure remains situated over the Lower Great Lakes \nand continues to dominate eastern Kentuckys weather. However, \nfarther upstream, an ~1000 mb surface low is projected over the \nnorthern Plains while its associated cold front trails southwestward \nover the Great Basin.\n\nThe upper-level ridging will continue to shift eastward through \nmidweek and gradually flatten as the upstream troughing traverses \nthe northern CONUS, such that by late in the week, quasi-zonal flow \nwith embedded shortwaves predominates over the Ohio Valley. At the \nsurface, the aforementioned low lifts northeast to near James Bay by \n00Z Thursday, where it becomes vertically stacked and weakens as it \ndrifts into Quebec. However, there should be enough upper-level \nenergy to push the systems associated cold front near the Ohio \nRiver. Shortwave ridging returns for Friday before another shortwave \ntrough approaches for the upcoming weekend. Model spread does \nincrease by the last days of the forecast period, leaving confidence \nin the details rather low.\n\nIn sensible weather terms, look for a fair and seasonably hot \nweather pattern for Tuesday and Wednesday, with highs in the mid to \nupper 80s and nighttime lows range mostly through the 60s. Shower \nand thunderstorm chances return on Thursday with the arrival of the \ncold front. Behind the front, slightly cooler conditions along with \ndiurnally modulated rain chances are then forecast heading into next \nweekend. Look for high temperatures to retreat to the lower to \nmiddle 80s by Friday and Saturday, while nighttime lows slip into \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s.",
